WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.350
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN: So
welcome to the advanced session

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.800
on Building a Cloud Native
Application from Scratch.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.540
I am Kavitha Radhakrishnan.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.270
I'm a senior PM on
Google Cloud, working

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.410
on the GCP Marketplace, as well
as Kubernetes applications.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.660
And I'll be speaking with
Kamil Hajduczenia, who

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.540
is a partner engineer
also on Google Cloud,

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.880
and he helps us get
our partners on board

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.040
into the GCP Marketplace.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.890
And, yes, I know our names
are hard to pronounce.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.027
That's how advanced
this session is.

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:39.398
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.210
Thank you.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.590
Today, we will cover the ways
Kubernetes applications greatly

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:51.930
improve the developer experience
for cloud native projects.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.730
We'll be doing a
very detailed demo

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.160
of building an app
from scratch on Anthos,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.140
our new hybrid and
multi-cloud platform,

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.860
and how GCP marketplace offers
open-source and commercial

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.900
solutions that accelerate
your development.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.970
When we think
about cloud native,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.890
we think about cloud
native technologies

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.540
that help enterprises build
and run scalable and secure

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.460
applications in the cloud.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.690
Containers, secure
mesh, microservices,

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:28.130
declarative APIs, these all
exemplify this approach.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.640
Many of our customers,
including many of you here,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.220
actually think
about the way they

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.985
modernized their applications
using containers.

00:01:36.985 --> 00:01:38.360
And so what we
found is that they

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.310
would start with
a few containers,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.830
and then they start to
scale those containers.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.960
And then when these
containers scale,

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.200
they turn to Kubernetes
as an orchestration engine

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.000
due to its ease of use,
portability, and manageability.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.040
Let's start with a
quick show of hands.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.250
How many of you are using
Container images today?

00:01:59.250 --> 00:01:59.750
OK.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:00.740
Fantastic.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.320
And how many of you are using
Kubernetes in production

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.450
right now?

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.560
It could be either on
prem or in the cloud.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.990
Great.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.520
That's a great number of you.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.440
So then I'm going to
assume that many of you

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.070
are familiar with core resources
like pods, persistent volumes,

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.910
and services that help you get
your workloads onto Kubernetes.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.430
And you're familiar with
the workloads resources

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.390
like deployments, ReplicaSets,
StatefulSets, and DaemonSets

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:30.230
for you to manage your stateless
and stateful workloads.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.510
But then we asked our
customers for feedback

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.330
as they were writing
these applications.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.980
And they told us
that it was really

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.770
hard to manage all of
these different concepts

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:41.700
and stitch them together.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.540
And if you do manage to
stitch them together, how

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.422
do you know that it will work?

00:02:46.422 --> 00:02:47.630
And will it continue working?

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.150
Especially as the ecosystem
is scaling and changing

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:51.990
so rapidly?

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.080
So we asked ourselves,
what if there

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.090
was a way for us to standardize
how we interact with these apps

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.430
without all of this complexity?

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.830
And that's how Kubernetes
apps were born.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.880
What we did is we worked with
sig apps and the open source

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.710
community on standardizing on
a application resource which

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:16.730
is a Custom Resource Definition,
or a CRD, that helps define

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.460
the core resources and
the workload resources

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.770
in your Kubernetes applications.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.030
This helps us package up these
resources as a single app.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.720
Packaging up as a single
app has multiple advantages.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.110
We're able to provide you,
then, with lifecycle management,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.080
debugging, monitoring,
and even building

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.400
out an ecosystem of apps that
we know will work well together.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.800
Thereby, making these
enterprise-ready

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.470
containerized solutions.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.640
We then went one step further.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.570
We saw that customers
were using health charts

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.390
for the deployments
of these applications.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.730
And so we went ahead and added
a deployer in the Marketplace,

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.340
in the GCP Marketplace,
that really helps you

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.490
deploy these apps anywhere
on prem or in the cloud.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.540
And so we've now created
these Kubernetes applications.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.640
We announced the beta last year.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.520
And these are available
in the GCP marketplace,

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.130
enterprise-ready
containerized solutions

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.740
with pre-built
deployment templates.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.870
We also made history
with this beta launch.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:23.510
We were the first
public cloud that

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.390
could support commercial
Kubernetes apps in the GCP

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.070
marketplace.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.420
But today is actually
a really exciting day,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.890
because I'm announcing
the general availability

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:38.122
of Kubernetes applications
in the GCP Marketplace.

00:04:38.122 --> 00:04:41.489
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.280
The GCP marketplace, for
those who may not know,

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:50.200
is a online curation,
a catalog of solutions

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.500
that we offer to enterprises.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.180
They can now just find
and buy solutions, just

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.490
like any other
marketplace, but it also

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.890
offers support for
management and deployment

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:01.450
of these solutions.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.480
We offer multiple features
within the GCP marketplace.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.690
One is we're one of
the few public clouds

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:12.190
that offer really popular open
source solutions packaged up

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.850
in the Marketplace.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.750
So, for instance, RabbitMQ,
which we will show in the demo

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.850
later on, or PostgresSQL,
that is also in the demo.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.430
One Google bill-- so it doesn't
matter what kind of solution

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.590
you're using in the
Marketplace, whether it's

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:32.350
SAAS or datasets or
Kubernetes apps or VMs,

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.320
any enterprise that is working
with the GCP Marketplace

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.540
gets one single bill
at the end of the day.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.790
And US customers can
just start to use,

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.690
if you're a developer
that's using this,

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:44.515
you can just start to use these
products within the Marketplace

00:05:44.515 --> 00:05:46.270
as well.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.600
Flexible pricing-- we
know that developers

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.270
want to be able to have
the flexibility of being

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:53.930
able to pay as you go.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:55.580
And so the ability
for you to really

00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:00.160
be flexible with your use but
also offering subscriptions

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.880
for more predictable usage.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.640
So if you want to
be able to use those

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.170
over a monthly or
annual subscriptions,

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.470
which is going to
be out in beta soon.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.920
But in addition, I also want
to announce some new features

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.230
as part of the
general availability.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.290
The first is that these
apps work with Anthos,

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:20.410
and we'll cover a little bit
more detail what this means.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.960
But it really opens up the
world for these applications

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.650
to be available, build
once, run anywhere,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:30.796
to be able to be supported on
hybrid as well as multi cloud.

00:06:30.796 --> 00:06:34.000
Cloud agnostic metering--
what this means

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.670
is that we actually
follow the app regardless

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.660
of where the app is deployed.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:44.200
So let's say you are deploying
onto GCP or into another cloud.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.890
You will still get metering,
so you can price and know

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:51.440
that your application is
being priced correctly.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.320
We also offer custom
price metrics.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.890
So, for instance, if you're
building a CI/CD application,

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:00.310
then maybe you want to price
on, say, the number of builds

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.140
or the number of deployments.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.520
But if you are now building
a database-based solution,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.120
then you can customize your
pricing just on storage.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.600
And so we're very flexible about
offering you those features.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.810
And the last-- managed
updates, which is out in alpha

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.902
now, one of the top asks.

00:07:18.902 --> 00:07:22.520
And so I'm going to dig into
that just a little bit more.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.690
We're introducing this
because our customers told us

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.600
that it was really
painful for users

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.980
to be able to create
these applications

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:32.780
and then push them
out to customers,

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.790
and then not having a great way
to update the feature updates

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:39.790
or even if you want to fix
security vulnerabilities.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:41.860
But instead of just
doing upgrades,

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.570
we've also added health checks.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.180
So we do auto
rollback when there's

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:49.180
auto triggers that can
really push a rollback

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.710
to a version that works.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:54.400
And so your customers are really
protected from any changes

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.410
that you may not
be able to control.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:08:00.050
We've also added UI and CLI
support in the Marketplace,

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:02.940
so you can push these updates
at any cadence that you want to.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.520
You've all seen a
version of this slide

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.950
at one of your
sessions until now,

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.360
but I just wanted
to call out why

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.690
this is such an important
part of the GCP Marketplace

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:16.960
strategy.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.710
So Anthos really opens
up the world for us

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:23.520
to be building apps that you
build once and run anywhere.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.470
And so for us to
be able to support

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:28.440
this in the marketplace--
so we have third party apps

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.070
that are in the marketplace
today that work with Anthos.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.990
And you can start to
use them, and you'll

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.720
see that in the demo.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.900
This vision has attracted
many enterprise partners

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.510
to our ecosystem.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.820
Here's just a quick look
at some of those partners.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.970
You will recognize some
really familiar names,

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.640
you will also see some
really exciting startups,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.340
but you will also see some
really popular open source

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.460
solutions that Google
has packaged up

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.580
and is making available on
the marketplace, as well.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.160
These are also in
multiple fields.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.660
And if you take a
quick look, you'll

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.500
see they're across security,
database, storage and machine

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.550
learning, logging, monitoring,
networking, and much more.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:15.150
So, really, expanding
to a base that meets

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.750
the needs of any enterprise.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.850
And also excited to
say that many of these

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.210
are available on-prem
today with Anthos.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.498
A huge thank you
to these partners.

00:09:25.498 --> 00:09:27.540
They've been working very
closely and really hard

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.900
over the last few weeks to
make this moment possible.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.140
You will see some of these
particular apps in the demo.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:39.976
Speaking of which, let me please
invite Kamil up on the stage.

00:09:39.976 --> 00:09:42.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:47.397 --> 00:09:48.730
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: Hi, everyone.

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.330
I'm Kamil Hajduczenia.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.960
I'm a partner engineer at
Google, which practically means

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.140
that I work with
our awesome partners

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:00.070
to build some cool products
for GCP Marketplace.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:05.020
And today, I'm going to show
you a few tips and tricks

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:08.080
that I use in everyday work.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.502
Kavitha, how can I
actually help you today?

00:10:10.502 --> 00:10:12.460
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN:
That's a great question.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.740
We could have built
any application

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:18.280
or used any use case, but we've
picked a broad enterprise use

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:22.450
case that covers product
and finance and marketing.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.880
But you can use this to
extrapolate to any use

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.840
cases you might have.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:30.010
So before I start, let
me transform myself

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.830
with these glasses into
a business analyst.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:36.460
And I'm now speaking
to my app developer.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:41.500
So, hey, Kamil, we need to build
a custom metrics collection

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:46.490
app that collects metric
streams from all over the world.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:48.550
And this data cannot fail.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.350
This is absolutely critical
data for our business.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:55.600
And also remember that
the US team is on GCP,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:58.073
but the India team
is on another cloud.

00:10:58.073 --> 00:10:58.990
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: OK.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:00.970
So very interesting use case.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.670
So let's start with those
multiple systems that want

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:06.860
to connect to our application.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.800
We don't have full
control over them.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.520
Probably some of them are
going to use some special data

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:16.220
format or custom
authentication mechanisms.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:19.060
So we could actually
start with the REST API.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:22.990
The REST API can be managed by
an API gateway, like Apigee,

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.330
and it will allow us
to easily integrate

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:27.770
multiple systems together.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.280
You also mentioned that you
want to run our application

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:33.490
in different environments,
like different clouds,

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.850
so we want our application
to be portable and have

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.050
a scalable ecosystem.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.860
So let's start with Kubernetes.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.360
Kubernetes sounds
like a great fit.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.840
It can run anywhere on premise
and on different clouds.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.670
And, actually, with
Anthos that you mentioned,

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:51.850
we can take
advantage of managing

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.710
those different Kubernetes
clusters from a single point,

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:56.260
which is GCP.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.825
So tell me something more
about those spikes in traffic.

00:11:59.825 --> 00:12:00.950
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN: Yes.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:03.820
So see, the marketing team
runs campaigns and events

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:04.670
constantly.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.430
And so sometimes they
collect new customers,

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.613
which can really make the
data spike dramatically.

00:12:10.613 --> 00:12:11.530
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: OK.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.180
So it means that our
application needs

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:18.790
to be able to be scalable, to
dynamically react to increasing

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:21.130
demands that we may see.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.010
At the same time, we
want to save money,

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:26.230
and whenever the
traffic goes down,

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:30.040
we want to have an
ability to scale down.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.860
You also mentioned that we
don't want to lose any data.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:33.380
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN:
That's right.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.672
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: So whenever
we are scaling or facing

00:12:35.672 --> 00:12:40.300
any spikes, we probably want
to use a message broker.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:44.110
A message broker will actually
persist coming requests

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:47.953
and let the back end handle
them whenever it's possible.

00:12:47.953 --> 00:12:50.620
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN: A message
broker sounds like a great fit,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.810
but don't forget the
CIO is really concerned

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:54.773
about the data and the
security of that data.

00:12:54.773 --> 00:12:55.690
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: OK.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.660
I wouldn't be that much
worried about security.

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:00.970
We can use Istio.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.300
Istio will be used for
increasing the security thanks

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.630
to the different
configurations that we can use.

