system design.

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.770
So we started with the REST API.

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:41.440
So we have different
clients that

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:44.800
are connecting to our system
through a REST API service.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.920
It must be backed up by
some component that is

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.850
able to handle some logic here.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:53.350
So we'll have a
deployment for our API.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.630
By its nature, it's
extremely scalable

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.040
because it's stateless.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:01.420
So let's take advantage of that
and attach a horizontal pod

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:06.790
autoscalar that can take care of
automated scaling up and down.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.320
At the same time, we know
that some of the data

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:10.195
need to be persisted.

00:14:10.195 --> 00:14:11.487
KAVITHA RADHAKRISHNAN: Correct.

00:14:11.487 --> 00:14:13.870
KAMIL HAJDUCZENIA: So we have
a separate deployment that

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.120
will serve as our back end.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:19.240
And it has it's own
horizontal pod autoscalar

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.420
because by its nature
it scales differently

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.250
than the stateless API.

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:30.250
So we want to take independent
configuration here.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.680
At the same time, we want
to have a database where

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.500
we'll persist the information.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.340
We don't want any
vendor lock in.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:40.640
We want to have solutions that
will be able to run anywhere.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.430
So in this particular
case, I will

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.560
